The fallback to the most recently used (MRU) account when an `accessToken` is provided but not found in the pool can lead to "hijacking" of requests. If a user provides their own OpenAI API key (not managed by the pool), and a pool account happens to be in a cooldown state, `maybeRotateBeforeOpenAIFetch` will incorrectly identify the request as belonging to the MRU pool account and replace the user's key with a pool token. The MRU fallback should only occur if no token was provided at all (e.g., during initial session startup).

```suggestion
  if (accessToken) {
    return accounts.find((account) => account.access === accessToken) || null;
  }
  return [...accounts].sort((a, b) => new Date(b.lastUsed || 0) - new Date(a.lastUsed || 0))[0] || null;
```

