![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current logic for parsing `retry-after` is slightly fragile. `parseInt(raw, 10)` on an ISO date string (e.g., "2024-05-20...") will return the year (2024), which `Number.isFinite` will accept as a valid number of seconds. This leads to an incorrect cooldown calculation. It's safer to explicitly check if the string is purely numeric before treating it as seconds.

```javascript
  if (/^\d+$/.test(raw)) {
    const seconds = parseInt(raw, 10);
    return Math.max(seconds * 1000, DEFAULT_OPENAI_COOLDOWN_MS);
  }
  const date = Date.parse(raw);
  if (Number.isFinite(date)) return Math.max(date - Date.now(), DEFAULT_OPENAI_COOLDOWN_MS);
  return DEFAULT_OPENAI_COOLDOWN_MS;
```

